body:
## Summary

In a React Native release build with `reactCompiler` enabled, a component that reads external state through `useSyncExternalStore` renders a stale value indefinitely. The store's subscriber fires and the version advances, but the rendered output never updates for the lifetime of the app session. The same component in the same build behaves correctly when the compiler is disabled, and a sibling component using plain `useState` updates correctly in the *same* compiled build.

This does not reproduce in a development build, only in a release (production) build.

## Repro

Fresh `create-expo-app` (SDK 57, RN 0.86.2, React 19.2.3), `expo-router` tabs, `expo.experiments.reactCompiler: true`.

Two tab screens. Screen A is the control:

```tsx
// app/(tabs)/index.tsx  — control, works
export default function LocalScreen() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  return (
    <View>
      <Text>{count}</Text>
      <Pressable onPress={() => setCount((c) => c + 1)}><Text>increment</Text></Pressable>
    </View>
  );
}
```

Screen B reads external state the documented way:

```tsx
// app/(tabs)/context.tsx  — renders stale
let version = 0;
let stored = 0;
const listeners = new Set<() => void>();
const subscribe = (l: () => void) => { listeners.add(l); return () => { listeners.delete(l); }; };
const getVersion = () => version;

function write() {
  stored += 1;
  version += 1;
  for (const l of listeners) l();
}

/** Opaque read of mutable external state (stands in for a SQLite query). */
function readStored(): number {
  return stored;
}

export default function ContextScreen() {
  const v = useSyncExternalStore(subscribe, getVersion, getVersion);
  const value = useMemo(() => {
    void v;
    return readStored();
  }, [v]);

  return (
    <View>
      <Text>{value}</Text>
      <Pressable onPress={write}><Text>write</Text></Pressable>
    </View>
  );
}
```

Build for the iOS simulator in release configuration and tap `write` twice on screen B.

## Expected

The number increments, as it does with the compiler disabled.

## Actual

The number stays at its initial value for the whole session. Tapping `increment` on screen A in the same build works normally, so touch handling and rendering are fine.

## Evidence

Each row is machine-graded: cold-start the app, tap, then compare the painted pixels against the stored value.

| Build | Screen A (`useState`) | Screen B (`useSyncExternalStore`) |
|---|---|---|
| release, `reactCompiler: true` | paints (0 → 2) | **stale, 3/3 sessions** |
| release, `reactCompiler: false` | paints | paints |

## Where this came from

Found in a real app, not synthesised. A local-first app reading SQLite through a store subscription showed every tab screen freezing in release builds: writes landed in the database and the UI kept painting the previous value for the entire session. Machine-graded probes on that app: **0/6 sessions painted with the compiler on, 6/6 with it off**, on otherwise identical code.

In the real app the failure had a specific signature that may help diagnosis: the UI rendered exactly **one version behind** (select Saturday, see Monday; select Sunday, see Saturday), which looks like a stale snapshot rather than a skipped render.

## Environment

- expo 57.0.13, expo-router 57.0.13
- react-native 0.86.2 (New Architecture / Fabric), react 19.2.3
- babel-plugin-react-compiler via `expo.experiments.reactCompiler`
- iOS simulator, release configuration, built on EAS
- Reproduces on every cold start; never reproduces in a development build

## Notes

`eslint-plugin-react-hooks@7` with the compiler diagnostics enabled (`purity`, `set-state-in-render`, `refs`, `immutability`, `globals`) reports the repro as clean, so there is no lint signal pointing at this.
